Check a `.bhive`'s signatures, and that every cell is what was signed, read to the end as
`archive.test` reads it. It passes when no signature failed and one is valid: from each
signer `require_signer` names, or without it from someone you trust (yourself, a contact,
or a certificate the system trusts). Fails with `SignatureRequired` when the archive isn't
signed that way or changed after it was signed, and `Corrupt` when a cell doesn't match
the digest that was signed.

Usage: beezip verify [OPTIONS] <ARCHIVE>

Arguments:
  <ARCHIVE>
          The archive

Options:
  -p, --password <PASSWORD>
          The password or recovery key, for an encrypted archive none of your keys opens

      --require-signer <SIGNER>
          Signed, validly, by each of these: a `bzsig1…` key, a certificate file, or the name of a
          signing key or contact. Matched by their whole SHA-256, never a fingerprint

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --output <FORMAT>
          How to print results; ndjson streams every job event

          Possible values:
          - human:  Text for people
          - json:   One JSON document on stdout: the output, or `{"error": {...}}`
          - ndjson: Every job event as a JSON line on stdout, ending with `Finished`, `Failed` or
            `Cancelled`
          
          [default: human]

  -i, --identity <FILE>
          A key file that opens encrypted .bhive archives (age identities, PEM and PKCS #8 keys,
          .pfx files on Windows); repeatable

      --password-stdin
          Read the password or recovery key from the first line of stdin, where other programs can't
          see it as they can the command line

  -q, --quiet
          No progress bars or info messages; warnings still print
